 "text": "A star about 30 million light years away keeps erupting like a supernova and surviving. At least  27 bursts between 2012 and 2026. They call it a supernova imposter. Why? Chandra caught it at  least 100 times brighter in x-rays than before its outburst began. The best explanation? A hidden  black hole or neutron star pulling material off it every 113 days. It is the first x-ray detection  Voyager 1 reaches one light day from Earth Wednesday, November 18th.  Follow Downlink Cat for that milestone.",
